# SYNOPSIS

```ndb
match=pci vendor=0x1af4 device=0x1042 program=/boot/bin/drv-virtio-blk
    post=disk# msi=1
```

```text
/srv/disk0, /srv/disk1, ...
```

# DESCRIPTION

`drv-virtio-blk` drives a virtio block device, as virtio 1.x §5.2 describes it, and serves it as a disk of the block class on its post in `/srv`.
It is the disk QEMU gives a guest, the boot disk among them.

devmgr(8) starts it for every PCI function 1af4:1042, the modern virtio block device, by the manifest in `/boot/drv/virtio-blk.ndb` shown above.
The driver gets the function's configuration space, its memory BARs, a DMA domain, one MSI-X interrupt (`msi=1`), for the request queue, and the listen end of its post, and nothing else; it takes no arguments and reads no options.
The post is numbered with the other disks in the order devmgr finds the functions on the bus, whichever driver serves them: `/srv/disk0` is the first disk found, virtio or NVMe.
partd(8) holds a connector to it, and hands out its partitions as posts of their own, which fsd(4) and the other file servers use.

At start it negotiates the features it uses: `VERSION_1`, `ACCESS_PLATFORM` when the device offers it, indirect descriptors, which it requires, and the device's segment limit, read-only flag, block size, flush and discard where offered.
It refuses, and exits, if the device is not a modern virtio device with MSI-X, lacks indirect descriptors, takes fewer than two segments a request, or has a request queue of fewer than 64 entries.
The sector size is the device's block size if that is a power of two from 512 to 4096, and 512 otherwise.

## Sessions

The block class is defined in `docs/proto/block.md`; in short:
a client sends `CONNECT` on the post, naming a window of the disk (a first sector and a count, 0 for the rest) and whether it is read-only, and gets back a ring session (see ring(2)) that reaches that window and nothing else.
Each request on the ring is one submission, completed by one completion with the same `user_data`, in any order.
The driver serves 8 sessions at once.
A `CONNECT` it cannot take is refused: `INVALID` for a message that is not one, `RANGE` for a window outside the disk, `NO_MEMORY` when 8 sessions are open.
On a device that says it is read-only, every session is read-only.

The client arena, 1 MiB, is given to the device through the DMA domain when the session opens, and every transfer is described to the device as the arena's own pages, so nothing is copied.
Each request in flight holds one of 64 slots, shared by all sessions: an indirect descriptor table, the request's header and its status byte, in memory the device reaches; the device sees one queue descriptor pointing at the table.
The requests:

: `INFO`
  Completes at once with the most bytes one transfer moves (128 KiB, or less if the device takes fewer segments: 4 KiB for each segment past the first), the sector size, the window's sectors, and the flags `INFO_READONLY` for a read-only session, `INFO_CACHE` if the device offers flush, and `INFO_DISCARD` if it offers discard.
: `READ`, `WRITE`
  A virtio-blk read or write of whole sectors, one segment for each physically contiguous run of the arena.
  A transfer that is empty, not a multiple of the sector size, not aligned to one in the arena, or longer than `INFO` says completes with `INVALID`; one outside the window or the arena with `RANGE`; a write in a read-only session with `ACCESS`; none of these reaches the device.
: `WRITE_FUA`
  virtio-blk has no FUA: a write, then, once it has succeeded, a flush on the same slot, and the request completes after both.
  Without flush it is a plain write.
: `FLUSH`
  A virtio-blk flush; it completes at once in a read-only session or when the device has no flush.
: `DISCARD`
  A virtio-blk discard of at most the device's `max_discard_sectors`; what lies beyond is left as it is, since a discard is a hint.
  It completes at once, as done, if the device cannot discard.

A device's I/O error completes the request with `IO`, and an unsupported request with `UNSUPPORTED`.
While every slot is busy the driver stops taking submissions, and takes them again as requests complete; sessions take slots in turn.
A session that does not drain its completions is dropped.
When a session goes with requests in flight, its arena stays given to the device until they complete.

If the driver exits, devmgr revokes its DMA domain, turns off bus mastering, resets the function where it can, and starts it again on the same post, with nothing kept; clients see their sessions close, dial again and submit again what had not completed (see `docs/proto/block.md` §4).
devmgr gives up after five starts.

# EXAMPLES

What devmgr and the drivers print at boot under QEMU, with the boot disk and a second disk of 64 MiB, and then after both drivers are killed:

```text
devmgr: 00:02.0 1af4:1042 storage msi-x bar1=4K bar4=16K
devmgr: 00:03.0 1af4:1042 storage msi-x bar1=4K bar4=16K
devmgr: started drv-virtio-blk
drv-virtio-blk: 66 MiB, 512-byte sectors, write cache, discard
devmgr: started drv-virtio-blk
drv-virtio-blk: 64 MiB, 512-byte sectors, write cache, discard
devmgr: /boot/bin/drv-virtio-blk exited: killed
devmgr: started drv-virtio-blk
```

